 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "A shorter way to say negative verbs"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "• A shorter way to say a negative verb is to use 「ん」 instead of 「ない」."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "知る → 知らない → 知らん"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "• Exceptions:"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "1. する → せん 2. くる → こん"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "1. すまん。\nSorry."
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "2. 韓国人と結婚しなくてはならん。\nYou must marry a Korean!"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "3. そんなことはさせん。\nI won't let you do such a thing!"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "You can even use this slang for past tense verbs by adding 「かった」."
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "• 皆、今日行くって、知らんかったよ。\nI didn't know everybody was going today."
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "A classical negative verb that ends in 「ぬ」"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Vocabulary"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "1. する (exception) - to do"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "2. 来る【く・る】(exception) - to come"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "3. 知る【し・る】(u-verb) - to know"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "4. 韓国人【かん・こく・じん】- Korean person"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "5. 結婚【けっ・こん】- marriage"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "6. なる (u-verb) - to become"
  },
  {
   "type": "text",
   "text": "7. 模擬【も・ぎ】- mock"
  }
 ]
}